# Design system — Sleek Paperstyle

Rulebook for the logandaim.at visual identity. Not history: follow it literally. Values live in
[`frontend/src/app/globals.css`](../../frontend/src/app/globals.css) only; this file names tokens and
states rules, never hex codes.

**Scope: appearance only.** Describes how any screen looks, feels, behaves visually — not what the
product does, which journeys it supports, or how data flows. Content decisions live in product specs.

## The identity

A warm, tactile logbook on paper, with the speed and precision of a modern web app. Borrows the
material language of uncoated paper and pencil ruling, nothing else.

- **Restrained skeuomorphism.** Flat paper, hairline rules, organic warmth, generous space. No
  leather, embossing, 3D book spines, ornament.
- **Utility first.** Fast, accessible, logical. Aesthetic never competes with reading or entry.
- **Calm focus.** Surface stays quiet so content is loudest.

## Do / Don't

Shortest complete statement of the identity. Wins over any rule below that disagrees.

| ✅ DO | ❌ DON'T |
|-------|----------|
| Use `--bg-surface` (`#FDFBF7`) and `--bg-canvas` (`#F4F1EA`) for warm paper. | Cold tech white as page background; blue-cast grey (Tailwind Slate) as neutral. |
| Use `--color-primary` (`#1B3B2B`, hunter green) as the single accent. | Ornamental accents: brown buttons, gold gradients, multi-coloured badges. |
| Reserve `--semantic-impact` (`#A82B2B`) for impacts, critical errors, destructive actions. | Red for decoration or neutral diagram elements. |
| Thin borders (1px), corners nearly square (4–6px). | Strong drop shadows, glassmorphism (`backdrop-blur`), rounded cards (16px+). |
| Serif headings with clear sans-serif forms. | Handwriting, script, or cursive for user input — legibility wins. |
| Treat `--semantic-*` as meaning: colour appears only when something happened. | Semantic colour because a screen looked empty or dull. |

## Tokens

Source is `globals.css`. Every token defined for light and dark. Add a token only with an entry here.

**One name per colour.** Utility suffix = token suffix: strip `--color-` or `--bg-` and you have the
class. No aliases, no second vocabulary.

One deliberate exception: `--text-primary` (ink) would collide with the brand on `text-primary`, so
the design system gives "primary" to the single brand colour. Ink is **`text-ink`**: brand owns every
`primary` utility, ink is called ink.

| Token | Utilities | Meaning | Use for |
|----|----|----|----|
| `--bg-canvas` | `bg-canvas` | Desk the paper lies on | App shell, page bodies, sidebar |
| `--bg-surface` | `bg-surface` | A sheet of paper | Cards, tables, content, header |
| `--bg-elevated` | `bg-elevated` | A raised sheet | Inputs, dropdowns, dialogs |
| `--bg-surface-muted` | `bg-surface-muted` | A second sheet | Table headers, hover rows, skeletons, inactive tracks |
| `--border-subtle` | `border-subtle`, `bg-subtle`, `divide-subtle` | Pencil hairline | Dividers, card borders, table rules |
| `--border-strong` | `border-strong` | A field's contour | Inputs at rest, millimetre grid |
| `--border-control` | `border-control` | A control's boundary, ≥ 3:1 | Inputs, selects, textareas, search fields, checkbox, radio ring, switch |
| `--text-primary` | `text-ink`, `bg-ink` | Warm deep ink (`#2C2A29`), never pure black | Body, headings, default icons |
| `--text-secondary` | `text-secondary` | Secondary ink | Labels, subtitles, metadata, units |
| `--text-muted` | `text-muted` | Muted ink | Placeholders, disabled only |
| `--color-primary` | `bg-primary`, `text-primary`, `border-primary`, `ring-primary`, `outline-primary` | **The one brand colour: hunter green** | One primary button per screen, active nav, focus rings, neutral vector drawings |
| `--color-primary-hover` | `bg-primary-hover` | Pressed primary | Primary button hover/active |
| `--color-primary-subtle` | `bg-primary-subtle` | Green wash | Subtle badges, selected rows |
| `--primary-foreground` | `text-primary-foreground` | Paper on hunter green | Labels on `--color-primary` |
| `--semantic-impact` | `text-impact`, `bg-impact`, `border-impact`, `outline-impact`, `border-l-impact` | Crimson | Impact points, critical deviations, delete, errors |
| `--semantic-warning` | `text-warning`, `bg-warning`, `border-warning` | Ocher / amber | Warnings, unsaved changes, moderate bands |
| `--semantic-success` | `text-success`, `bg-success`, `border-success` | Forest green | Optimal band, confirmations, positive trends |
| `--semantic-info` | `text-info`, `bg-info`, `border-info` | Steel blue | Meteorological data, distance helper lines only |

### Mechanics

| Token | Meaning | Use for |
|----|----|----|
| `--shadow-paper` | Sheet shadow | `paper` class. Soft in light, `none` in dark |
| `--shadow-overlay` | Floating-sheet shadow | `paper-overlay` class: dialogs, menus, tooltips |
| `--font-sans` | Interface face: **Inter** | Menus, tables, forms, figures. Never name a family in a component |
| `--font-serif` | Book face: **Lora** | Titles only — page, card, dialog headings |
| `--font-mono` | Machine values only | References, base URLs, token names, file names. Never prose |

Lora is the book face, Inter the work face. Add a weight to `layout.tsx` before using it, or the
browser synthesises it. Geist Mono is the machine face.

Dark mode is class-based (`@custom-variant dark`), defaulting to `prefers-color-scheme`. The gallery
sets `.light` / `.dark` on wrappers to show both side by side.

## Colour discipline

One brand colour. Everything else is paper, ink, or explicit meaning:

- Hunter green: brand, primary action, active state. Nothing else.
- Paper and warm neutrals: all structure.
- Impact, warning, success, info: meaning only, never decoration. Always paired with a word.

`--semantic-impact` is the most restricted. It means "this is an impact", "this failed", or "this
destroys something". Never a hover accent, chart series, category marker, or a way to liven a screen.
`--semantic-info` is reserved for meteorological data and distance helper lines, not general
"informational" accents.

## Contrast exceptions

Four prescribed values fail WCAG 2.2 AA as text. Kept because prescribed; each restricted to where
it is legible:

| Value | Ratio on paper | Restriction |
|----|----|----|
| `--text-muted` `#9E9A93` | 2.48:1 | Placeholders, disabled only. Never body, labels, metadata. |
| `--semantic-warning` `#D97706` | 3.08:1 | Rules, washes, icons, borders. A warning that must be *read* is ink on the amber wash. |
| `--border-strong` `#CFCAC0` | 1.58:1 | 1px contour, never a text carrier. |
| `--semantic-success` `#15803D` | 4.45:1 canvas, 4.85:1 surface | Text on `--bg-surface` only, never the darker canvas. |

Rule: **a semantic colour may always be a mark, rule, or wash; may be text only where the table
allows.** Badge and Alert encode this — `warning` is ink on amber, not amber text.

A control's boundary is a separate bar: WCAG 2.2 §1.4.11 wants **3:1** for the outline of a control,
so every control boundary uses `--border-control`, never `--border-strong` — not just the drawn ones:
inputs, selects, textareas, and search fields are covered by the same rule. A segmented `ToggleGroup`
is the exception: its cells are adjacent labelled buttons, not one field, so the strip stays on
`border-subtle` and a heavier contour reads as framed furniture.

## Paper language

Six elements carry the identity. Use them; do not invent a seventh.

1. **The sheet.** Content sits on `Card` — hairline and a whisper of shadow, never a heavy border or
   elevation. Dark mode: shadow `none`, hairline alone separates.
2. **The serif title.** Page, card, dialog titles are `font-serif`. Serif is the book face and the
   only material character; body never uses it.
3. **The ruled line.** `Separator` draws `rule-ticks`: small ticks along a hairline like printed
   form ruling. Sections divided by a ruled line, never by empty space alone or a shadow.
4. **The fine contour.** Inputs/selects sit on `--bg-elevated` with a 1px `--border-strong` contour,
   turning `--color-primary` on focus, darkening on hover. Card hairline is `--border-subtle`; a
   field contour is stronger because that boundary is functional.
5. **The stamp.** `Badge` is a rubber stamp: hairline box, tracked small caps, semantic tint. Status
   always carries its word.
6. **The millimetre grid.** Charts/diagrams are thin geometric lines on a faint grid — drawn from
   `--border-subtle` at reduced strength, never filled shapes or gradients.
7. **The hachure wall.** `HatchBand` draws diagonal `///////` strokes as the default separation
   between major sections: a change of subject without a border around anything. `dense` for the
   break before a footer. `Perforation` marks a separable annex instead.
8. **The page furniture.** `SheetNumber` (margin plate number), `RegisterLine` (running head),
   `MarginNote` (a remark at the margin, never an `Alert`), `RulerEdge` (a block stating its own
   scale), `DogEaredSheet` (already read), `StampMark` (record-level verdict). All decorative;
   none may be the only carrier of state.

## Rules

1. **Semantic utilities only, one name per colour.** Use `bg-surface`, `text-secondary`,
   `border-subtle`, `bg-primary`, `text-impact`. Never a raw palette class (`bg-stone-100`,
   `text-green-800`, `border-red-200`) or a hex value in a component. The shadcn-era names
   (`text-foreground`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`, `text-destructive`,
   `bg-surface-elevated`, `bg-background`, `outline-ring`) are **retired**; `check:design` fails if
   any reappears — the palette must not grow a second vocabulary. No alias layer: `@theme inline`
   maps each token to exactly one utility. Paper patterns come from `rule-ticks`, `rule-ticks-quiet`,
   `leader`, `select-chevron`, `checkbox-tick`; their SVG masks live in `globals.css` and inherit
   `currentColor` instead of hardcoding a stroke. An inline style may reference only `var(--…)`.
2. **Contrast is a gate.** Text meets WCAG 2.2 AA (4.5:1 normal). The gallery measures every
   documented pair; a change dropping one below AA is not accepted. The four exceptions above are
   role-restricted, not relaxed.
3. **Colour is never the only signal.** Status and validation carry meaning in text or icon too.
   Badges and alerts always include the word.
4. **Touch targets ≥ 44×44 px** on every interactive element. Target ≠ drawn control: a control
   keeps its row height and extends its hit area with a `before` overlay. Never make a control
   taller than its row to reach 44px.
5. **Focus always visible.** `focus-visible:outline-2` with `outline-primary`. Never remove an
   outline without an equally visible replacement.
6. **One primary action per screen.** Everything else `secondary` or `ghost`. Destructive uses
   `destructive`, never the primary button.
7. **German UI copy, English code.** Labels German, identifiers and comments English. Do not
   hardcode new copy in a component beyond gallery examples; product wording lives with the product.
8. **Spacing scale** is Tailwind default (4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32 px). Page bodies and cards `p-6`,
   fields stack with `space-y-1.5` inside `Field`. White space is part of the identity: prefer a
   wider gap to another rule.
9. **Variants come from `cva`.** Add variants in the component's `cva` definition, never by passing
   arbitrary classes at call sites. Public variant names stay backwards compatible.
10. **Flat by default.** Edges from `border-subtle`. Cards carry `paper` (`none` in dark); only
    genuinely floating overlays (dialogs, dropdowns, tooltips) carry `paper-overlay`. No hover
    elevation, gradients, glass. A dragging row is `ring-1 ring-primary`, never a shadow.
11. **Angular paper, not pills.** `--radius` 4–6px: controls `rounded-sm`, containers `rounded-md`.
    Pills only where the shape is the meaning (progress track, switch track, radio dot). Rounding is
    never decoration.
12. **Serif for titles only.** `font-serif` on page, card, dialog headings; never body, table cells,
    form labels, buttons. Never script or cursive — input must stay legible.
13. **Warm and natural, low saturation.** Warm paper and ink; dark is warm charcoal, never pure
    black, never cold blue-grey. Status colours distinguishable without glowing.

## Components

Primitives live in `frontend/src/components/ui/`. shadcn-style: `class-variance-authority` for
variants, Radix where behaviour is non-trivial, `cn` (`clsx` + `tailwind-merge`) for merging.

- Only `Button`, `Alert`, `Badge` carry status variants. Do not invent colour variants elsewhere.
- `Card` is a paper sheet, carries `paper`. `CardTitle` is `font-serif`; `CardHeader` closes with a
  `rule-ticks-quiet` hairline so the title block reads as a printed form head.
- `Badge` is a stamp: hairline, tracked caps, tinted, names its status in words. `primary` is the one
  stamp in brand ink — use for the single state worth marking strongly.
- `Alert` is a margin note: coloured left rule, tinted wash, never a full coloured block. `error`
  uses `role="alert"`.
- `Separator` draws the ruled line; standard section divider.
- `Input`, `Textarea`, `Select`, `SearchInput` sit on `--bg-elevated` with a `--border-control`
  contour turning `--color-primary` on focus, darkening on hover. `Select` stays native on purpose:
  the platform picker survives touch and one-handed use. Its chevron is a `currentColor` mask, so it
  flips with the theme.
- `Checkbox` is a square with `checkbox-tick`: green fill, paper-coloured tick, both from tokens.
  Its contour is `border-2`, not a hairline.
- **Inked controls.** Anything drawn rather than typed carries a strong contour, because a mark on
  paper is inked: `Checkbox` and `Switch`'s track and thumb are `border-2`, `RadioGroupItem` is
  `border-[3px]`. A form must read as marks, not browser chrome.
- That contour is `border-control`, not `border-strong`. `--border-strong` is a 1px contour at
  1.58:1 and may never bound a control: a control's boundary is meaningful and needs **3:1**
  (WCAG 2.2 §1.4.11). `--border-control` measures 5.20:1 light and 3.82:1 dark. Thickening a
  low-contrast hairline does not make it pass; the colour has to change.
- `RadioGroupItem` is a check circle: a `border-[3px]` ring on a 20px circle that fills with a solid
  green disc held off the ring by a paper gap, not a small dot in a large hole. The ring is heavier
  than the other controls because at 2px on 18px it read as a default browser radio.
- `Slider`'s bullet is fully round (`rounded-full`) with `border-2 border-primary` and a solid ink
  centre, so it reads as a cursor riding the ruled track. A squared knob would read as a second tick.
  `CheckpointSlider`'s bullet is the same shape and centre, contoured in ink rather than primary
  because it sits on a ramp of caller-supplied stake colours and ink survives all of them.
- Roundness stays reserved for meaning: the slider bullet, the radio ring, the switch track, and the
  radio dot. Everything else is `rounded-sm`/`rounded-md`.
- `Table` is a ruled ledger: hairlines between rows, no zebra banding, `tabular-nums`, right-aligned
  figures. `TableKeyValue` renders label · dotted-leader · value pairs for metric lists.
- `Progress` is a measured band with fifth-ticks; keeps `rounded-sm` — a progress track's roundness
  would mean nothing.
- `Slider` is a value input drawn as a ruled measure: compact squared knob on a divided track, 44px
  hit area around it. Do not enlarge the visible knob to reach the touch minimum.
- `CheckpointSlider` is that input for a scale with named checkpoints: track divided into coloured
  bands between them. **Band colours are caller-supplied data**, not design tokens — they arrive as
  a `checkpoints` prop. The design system owns no palette for them and must never add one to
  `globals.css`. Callers pass `var(--…)` references, never hex. Value moves freely, never snaps.
  Bands are `aria-hidden`; the legend repeats every checkpoint by name, the thumb's `aria-valuetext`
  names the nearest. No checkpoints renders a plain track.
- Sortable `Table` rows use `@dnd-kit`. Drag activator is a dedicated handle, never the whole row;
  hit area follows the touch rule. Handle names distinct per row, never inventing a position that
  does not exist. Numbers `tabular-nums` and right-aligned; machine values mono. Table heads tracked
  small caps on `--bg-surface-muted`.
- `Field` owns label, hint, error placement. Use it instead of hand-assembling label + input.
- Layout (`app-shell`, `sidebar`, `page-chrome`) and workflow components compose from primitives.
  Active nav item is a filled treatment plus `aria-current`, never colour alone.

## Screen composition

A screen is a page of the logbook. **Appearance only.** Whether a journey shows a map, a progress
bar, or a step list is a product decision, not settled here.

1. Page header: serif title with a hairline ruled line beneath.
2. Content blocks are `Card`s (sheets) or ruled table sections; a `Separator` between sections.
3. Dense and scannable: one primary action, tables for records, forms stacked in `Field`.
4. Numbers in rows right-aligned, `tabular-nums`. IDs, references, coordinates `--font-mono`.
5. Exactly one primary action per screen, in the first action row. Else secondary/ghost.
6. Status always as word (+ icon or stamp); colour only as second channel.

## The gallery at `/dev`

`frontend/src/app/dev/page.dev.tsx` exists only under `next dev`: `next.config.ts` adds `dev.tsx` /
`dev.ts` to `pageExtensions` only in the development phase, so `next build` never emits the route.

- Laid out as a printed specimen book: cover title block, table-of-contents index, numbered plates.
  Each `ComponentSection` carries a two-digit mono `index` in the margin, a serif title, a one-line
  rationale, and a ruled line before the specimens.
- Every plate renders twice — `.light` (Papier) and `.dark` (Lampe) — so a palette change is judged
  side by side, not from memory.
- Tokens plate reads computed CSS variables and prints the contrast ratio for every documented pair
  with an AA pass/fail stamp.
- Touch-target toggle outlines interactive elements to reveal targets under 44px. Dashed amber on
  purpose: a measuring overlay, not part of the identity.
- Datenbilder plate shows the drawing vocabulary as elements — diagram, series, scale, legend —
  deliberately without screens or flows, so the language can be judged alone.
- **The gallery is domain-free.** Fixtures use neutral German UI language (`Eintrag`, `Status`,
  `Kategorie`, `Fortschritt`, `Alpha`/`Beta`/`Gamma`), never product entities, shooting terms, or a
  real place name. It demonstrates *how* the product looks; *what* it does is decided in product
  specs, and a plate must not imply a journey.
- Earlier identity proposals removed. The gallery shows the accepted system only; a superseded
  direction belongs in the spec decision log, not the page.

Visual change workflow: edit `globals.css` or a component, look at `/dev`, decide, then write the
result into this rule and the spec decision log **in the same commit**.

## Keeping this rule in sync

- A new or renamed token, changed pairing, new component variant, or changed visual rule must land
  here and in `specs/product/visual-identity.md` in the same commit. No "document it later".
- `check:design` (in `mise run check`) fails when a token in `globals.css` is undocumented here, a
  raw palette class appears under `frontend/src` outside `globals.css`, or a retired utility name
  returns.
- Prose nuance cannot be checked mechanically. The "design rule and spec updated" PR checklist item
  is the human half.
